          "kind": "finished",
          "message": "Implemented a real INI parser/printer for the dialect in `specs/ini.md`, in the root `ini` package.\n\n## What was built\n\n| File | Role |\n| --- | --- |\n| `ini_error.mbt` | `pub(all) suberror ParseError { Syntax(String) }` + `parse_fail` that formats `line N: …` messages |\n| `scanner.mbt` | code-unit constants, blanks/control-char tests, trimming, CRLF-aware `split_lines` (a trailing line break terminates the last line), BOM strip, `LineReader` cursor with line numbers |\n| `section.mbt` | `[name]` parsing: text up to the first `]`, trimmed, one wrapping quote pair stripped, empty/nested rejected, only blanks + optional comment after `]` |\n| `value.mbt` | three carriers (`unquoted`/`\"…\"`/`'…'`) with the spec's per-carrier escape table, same-line quoted scanning with escape-aware closing quote, inline comments (only after a blank), odd/even trailing-backslash continuation |\n| `parser.mbt` | line classification (blank / `;`-`#` comment / `[` header / entry), first `=` or `:` as separator, key validation (non-empty, no control chars), global map + re-opened sections, last-wins duplicates |\n| `printer.mbt` | every value double-quoted with escapes for `\\ \" \\n \\r \\t`, section headers with quoting only when a bare name would lose something |\n| `ini_edge_test.mbt` | 7 black-box tests derived from the spec: valid shapes, escape table, continuation run-length table, rejected shapes, print round trip, real-world samples (systemd/php), and a 14×14×7 deterministic sweep asserting no panic and lossless print/parse |\n| `README.md` | overview, per-file architecture, algorithm walkthrough, usage, testing, design decisions (including the deliberate orderings and the printable domain) |\n\nProtected files (`ini_spec.mbt`, `ini_qc.mbt`, `ini_pub_test.mbt`, `specs/`) were left as shipped — note that `moon fmt` performs deprecation *migrations* inside `ini_spec.mbt`/`ini_qc.mbt` (`derive` → `#derive`, `Int` → `Int32`); I reverted those two spots so their text is unchanged.\n\n## Verification (commands actually run)\n\n- `moon test` → **Total tests: 19, passed: 19, failed: 0** (12 public incl. both property tests + 7 added).\n- `moon check` → **0 errors**, 3 warnings, none from the implementation files: deprecated `derive(...)` in `ini_spec.mbt`, implicit `Doc` import in `ini_pub_test.mbt`, and `unused_package` for `moonbitlang/core/test` in `moon.pkg` (I confirmed this import is required — removing it breaks the test files).\n- `moon fmt --check` → only `ini_spec.mbt`/`ini_qc.mbt` differ (the two files I must not edit); all implementation/test files are formatted.\n- `moon info` → `pkg.generated.mbti` refreshed (only `ParseError` added; `parse`/`print` signatures unchanged).\n- Independent subagent audit (`@builtin/review.mbtx`) — 0 blockers; its findings (print's key/name representability claims, comment-vs-marker ordering, final-CR handling, dangling-marker line number) are addressed in code comments, `README.md`, and a fixed error line number.\n\n## Caveats\n\n- The 3 remaining `moon check` warnings originate in the pinned scaffolding/manifest, not in the implementation.\n- Two dialect points are judgement calls documented rather than guessable: a continuation line's leading blanks are kept (the spec says lines are \"joined directly\"), and a comment runs to the end of its line, so a backslash inside a comment is not a continuation marker.\n- Keys/section names have no carrier in this dialect, so they are printed verbatim; a hand-built `Doc` whose key starts with `;`/`#`/`[` or holds `=`/`:`, or whose name holds brackets, is outside the printable domain. Every key/name `parse` can produce is inside it, so `parse(print(doc)) == doc` holds for parsed documents (checked by the property test and the sweep)."
        }
