        "stop_reason": {
          "kind": "finished",
          "message": "## Implemented: a general INI parser for this repo's dialect\n\n`moon test` → **90 tests, 90 passed** (12 given public + 78 I added), and the\nsame 90 pass on `wasm-gc`, `js` and `native`. `moon check` reports 0 errors;\nthe only warnings left are pre-existing ones inside the protected files\n(`ini_spec.mbt`'s `derive(...)`, `ini_pub_test.mbt`'s unqualified `Doc`,\n`moon.pkg`'s unused-`core/test` note).\n\n### Source layout (root `ini` package, ~695 lines of core code)\n\n| file | role |\n| --- | --- |\n| `errors.mbt` | private `IniError(line, message)` + generic `err` helper |\n| `chars.mbt` | blank/control classes, clamped index helpers, trailing-backslash counter |\n| `lines.mbt` | LF/CRLF physical-line splitting |\n| `sections.mbt` | header scanning: `[`…`]`, nested-bracket rejection, one wrapping quote pair |\n| `values.mbt` | `Carrier` enum + the three escape tables, quoted values, inline-comment scan |\n| `parser.mbt` | `Parser` line walk, first-separator split, key validation, continuations, `pub fn parse` |\n| `printer.mbt` | `pub fn print`: values in `\"…\"`, section names quoted only when needed |\n\n`ini_spec.mbt`, `ini_qc.mbt`, `specs/` and `ini_pub_test.mbt` are byte-identical\nto the delivered versions (`moon fmt` reformats the first two, so its hunks\nthere were reverted by hand; every other file is fmt-clean).\n\n### Behaviour implemented from `specs/ini.md`\nSections with the global map (spaces/unicode/punctuation/embedded quotes in\nnames, `[\"x\"]`/`['x']` unwrapping, re-open + last-wins), first `=`/`:` as\nseparator, key trimming with control-character rejection, `;`/`#` full-line and\ninline comments (marker needs a preceding blank, quoted values are exempt),\nper-carrier escapes (`\\\\ \\n \\r \\t`, `+\"`, `+'`), backslash continuations decided\nby counting the trailing run before decoding (the N=1..4 table), and rejection\nof unclosed/empty/nested headers, missing separators, empty keys, unterminated\nor mismatched quotes, text after a closing quote, and dangling markers.\n\n### Testing\nAdded `parse_wbtest`, `value_wbtest`, `continuation_wbtest`, `errors_wbtest`,\n`print_wbtest`, `realworld_wbtest` (git config / systemd / php.ini shapes) and\n`fuzz_wbtest` — the last parses **every string up to length 3 over the 15\nsyntactic characters** (plus a key and a section header wrapped around each)\nand checks the invariant \"accepted ⇒ `parse(print(doc)) == doc`\", then two\n`quickcheck` properties over arbitrary strings.\n\n### Audits\nTwo independent explore-agents audited the code against the spec and for\nrobustness. The conformance audit confirmed every escape-table cell, the\ncontinuation table, comment rules, section/key rules and the whole\nmust-reject list; it found one real printer defect — section names needing\nquotes that contain *both* quote characters were printed bare and re-parsed\ndifferently (sections could even merge). Fixed: names now always wrap in `\"`,\nwhich the header scanner strips exactly once, so any quote mix survives; two\nregression tests cover it. The robustness audit found no reachable panic,\nout-of-bounds index, non-terminating loop, or super-linear blow-up (100k-input\nsoup + ~300k exhaustive short strings). Remaining deliberate choices are\ndocumented in code comments and in `README.md`: blanks after a continuation\nmarker are insignificant; a comment after `]` is accepted; and keys/section\nnames that the dialect cannot express (separators in a key, brackets in a name,\nempty name) are written plainly — out of the printed round-trip contract, which\nonly covers dialect-valid keys and names.\n\n`README.md` documents the overview, module architecture, the parsing pipeline,\nimplementation notes, usage examples, how to run the tests, and the design\ntrade-offs."
